  "strategy": "Implementation Outline: 1. Parse inputs 2. INITIALIZE PARAMETERS (n, M) 3. VALIDATE PARENT 4. COARSE POPULATION EVOLUTION 5. COOLING PHASE 6. FINE-TUNE 7. FINAL EVALUATION\n\nDetailed Implementation Steps: 1. Approach and Rationale Start with Chebyshev functions to model h for translation invariance. Rationale: They minimize variation and support global shifts. Limitations: Plain Chebyshev may need perturbations for edge effects. L\u1ec1Instruction: Use only legal function names. 2. Mathematical Spec: - h(x) parameterized as sum a_j T_j(x) for j=0 to M. - T_j(x) normalized to [ -1,1] domain, then adjust to [0,2]. - Coefficients bounded per di Krooto feature. - Objective computed via oversampled grid. 3. Implementation: - Read seed, budget. - Define generate_candidate(), evolve_pop(), sa_update().   \u2013 Phase 1: Generate M-parameter candidates via Chebyshev with derivative-free Search, and uplift to n_points grid using np.interp, then project integral. - Evaluate via parent or initial_h_values auto-referenced. - Backup grid shape. - 4. Search Choices: M from 6 to com2, optimization time derivation. - Non-gradient: Default to 100-dimensional space. - 5. Budget: Compute time for Chebyshev evaluation. 6. Validation: Compute C5 with same mode=\"full\". Verify constraint.\n\nExample Calculation: For n=90, dx=2/90, hx np.linspace(0,2,90), project from M=10 coefficients. Use Chebyshev polynomials for T_j(x) = cos( j*arccos((2x/2)-1) ), etc.\n\nSafety Check: Parameters must stay within integral sum constraint.\n\nEnd Procedure.",
